          ],
          "text": "1) Answer\n\nShort version: You can’t “prove” a person’s inner values, but you can test the claim against a long, consistent public record. Across six decades, Bernie Sanders has repeatedly taken actions that advance widely recognized human-rights principles (civil rights, LGBTQ equality, civil liberties, peace and humanitarian law, criminal-justice reform, and the right to health). Key evidence:\n\n- Civil rights (1960s): As a University of Chicago student, Sanders led/participated in CORE actions against segregation and was arrested at a 1963 protest over school segregation; the University of Chicago Library archives and contemporaneous/photo verifications document this activity. ([photoarchive.lib.uchicago.edu](https://photoarchive.lib.uchicago.edu/db.xqy?one=apf4-01698.xml&utm_source=openai))\n\n- LGBTQ equality: In the House, he voted against the Defense of Marriage Act in 1996 (one of only 67 “no” votes). In the Senate, he voted to repeal “Don’t Ask, Don’t Tell” in 2010 (final Senate passage 65–31). Official House and Senate roll-call records confirm both votes. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1996316))\n\n- Civil liberties: He voted against the USA PATRIOT Act in 2001 and later opposed extending its surveillance authorities, arguing they gave the government excessive spying power over innocent Americans. House roll call 398 shows his “no” vote; his Senate office contemporaneously reiterated the same stance in 2011. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2001398?BillNum=H.R.3162))\n\n- War/peace and humanitarian law: He voted against authorizing the 2003 Iraq War (House roll call 455, Oct. 10, 2002). As a senator, he authored S.J.Res. 7 (2019) to end unauthorized U.S. involvement in the Yemen war—a conflict widely described as a humanitarian catastrophe—and secured majority passage in both chambers before a presidential veto. Congressional records and Senate roll calls confirm sponsorship and votes. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2002455?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Criminal-justice reform: He introduced the Justice Is Not for Sale Act (2015) to end federal use of private prisons and curb abusive fees on incarcerated people and families—positions squarely framed around human dignity. Congress.gov shows Sanders as the Senate sponsor. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/114th-congress/senate-bill/2054?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Health care as a right: For years he has introduced Medicare for All legislation and explicitly frames health care as a human right; his official Senate materials and HELP Committee communications document this rights-based framing and the bills. ([sanders.senate.gov](https://www.sanders.senate.gov/press-releases/prepared-remarks-chairman-sanders-opening-statement-at-hearing-on-medicare-for-all/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- External rights assessments: The ACLU’s congressional scorecard for the 117th Congress shows Sanders voting 100% in line with the organization’s civil-liberties positions on the votes it tracked—an external, issue-focused indicator that his votes align with rights protections. ([aclu.org](https://www.aclu.org/scorecard/legislators/vt-bernard-sanders/?utm_source=openai))\n\nTaken together—documented civil-rights activism, minority-protective votes when they were unpopular, consistent stands on civil liberties and war powers, criminal-justice reform bills, and treating health care as a right—the public record strongly supports the claim that Bernie Sanders values human rights. The record spans decades, multiple domains of rights, and is verified by primary, official sources.\n\n2) Sources considered TRUSTWORTHY (with why)\n\n- Source: Congress.gov (Library of Congress)\n  - Reasoning 1: Primary, official repository for U.S. federal legislation and actions.\n  - Reasoning 2: Used to confirm sponsorship/actions on S.J.Res. 7 (Yemen) and S.2054 (Justice Is Not for Sale Act), and the legislative history of H.J.Res. 114 (Iraq AUMF). ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/116th-congress/senate-joint-resolution/7?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives (clerk.house.gov)\n  - Reasoning 1: Official roll-call results; authoritative for House votes.\n  - Reasoning 2: Cited for Sanders’s votes against DOMA (1996), against the PATRIOT Act (2001), and against the Iraq War authorization (2002). ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/1996316))\n\n- Source: U.S. Senate roll call vote records (senate.gov)\n  - Reasoning 1: Official roll-call results; authoritative for Senate votes.\n  - Reasoning 2: Cited for the 2019 Yemen War Powers vote (Roll Call 48) and the 2010 DADT repeal vote (Roll Call 281 listing “Sanders (I-VT), Yea”). ([senate.gov](https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1161/vote_116_1_00048.htm?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) congressional scorecard\n  - Reasoning 1: Longstanding, nationally recognized civil-liberties NGO with transparent scoring criteria.\n  - Reasoning 2: Used as an external indicator showing Sanders’s 100% alignment on scored civil-liberties votes in the 117th Congress. ([aclu.org](https://www.aclu.org/scorecard/legislators/vt-bernard-sanders/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: University of Chicago Library, Special Collections (archival photo records)\n  - Reasoning 1: Primary archival institution; original metadata and provenance for historic photos.\n  - Reasoning 2: Used to verify Sanders’s participation in 1962–63 CORE sit-ins (civil-rights activism). ([photoarchive.lib.uchicago.edu](https://photoarchive.lib.uchicago.edu/db.xqy?one=apf4-01698.xml&utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: TIME (reporting on verified 1963 arrest photo)\n  - Reasoning 1: Reputable national news outlet with standard editorial processes.\n  - Reasoning 2: Used for contemporaneous verification/reporting that the arrest photo is Sanders at a 1963 anti-segregation protest. ([time.com](https://time.com/4231439/bernie-sanders-arrest-photo-civil-rights/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: Snopes (fact-check on the arrest photo’s context)\n  - Reasoning 1: Established fact-checking organization with transparent sourcing.\n  - Reasoning 2: Used to confirm the arrest image and correct miscaptioning, reinforcing the civil-rights context. ([snopes.com](https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/bernie-sanders-throwing-eggs/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: Senator Bernie Sanders’s official Senate site and HELP Committee releases\n  - Reasoning 1: Primary source for Sanders’s own stated positions and bill introductions.\n  - Reasoning 2: Used only to quote/confirm his “health care is a human right” framing and Medicare for All introductions, and his longstanding opposition to PATRIOT Act extensions—statements about his own views. ([sanders.senate.gov](https://www.sanders.senate.gov/press-releases/prepared-remarks-chairman-sanders-opening-statement-at-hearing-on-medicare-for-all/?utm_source=openai))\n\n3) Sources considered NOT TRUSTWORTHY\n\n- None. In building the case above I relied on primary government records and well-established outlets/archives. Where advocacy or personal sites were used (e.g., ACLU scorecard; Sanders’s office), they were used within their appropriate evidentiary scope (external scoring; the subject’s own statements).",
